  "uid": "421850.ZvHtK,JY65U0gSTUYqfeNrwTVNcpBcD17Q5N,E1QkGU=",
  "from_addr": "\"Damian Paletta, WSJ\" <access@interactive.wsj.com>",
  "subject": "WSJ Politics Special Edition: Democrats Are Closing With Chaos",
  "date": "Sun, 01 Nov 2026 15:10:01 -0500",
  "body_text": "96 Dysfunction in Michigan and mystery in Alaska mean party leaders have healing work to do—no matter the results on Tuesday &#8202; Is this email difficult to read? View in browser &#8202; &#8202; &#8202; &#8202; &#8202; &#8202; &#8202; &#8202; Damian Paletta &#8202; Democrats’ Disastrous Final Week Foreshadows Post-Election Drama &#8202; Dysfunction in Michigan and mystery in Alaska mean party leaders have healing work to do—no matter the results on Tuesday &#8202; &#8202; Former Rep. Mike Rogers, a Republican, and Rep. Haley Stevens, a Democrat, meet the press Sunday. EMILY ELCONIN/BLOOMBERG NEWS &#8202; Good morning. Maybe they win Tuesday. Maybe they win a whole lot. But the major problems Democrats revealed in the past week won’t go away even if there is a Blue Wave.\n\n &#8202; This election was never really about a united message from Democrats. Rather, it had turned into a referendum on President Trump, the Iran war, prices, and—to a certain extent—an antitech backlash.\n\n &#8202; But hoooooooo boy. In the past week, Democrats have put some of their problems on full display.\n\n &#8202; In no place has the dysfunction been as apparent as Michigan. Democratic Rep. Haley Stevens on Sunday endorsed Mike Rogers, the Republican candidate for Senate. This followed two days of “passive aggressive” and then finally “aggressive aggressive” squabbling between Stevens and Abdul El-Sayed, the Democratic Senate candidate.\n\n &#8202; El-Sayed barely edged Stevens in the primary earlier this year, and clearly their differences had never been mended. Their rift of new guard vs. old guard is something Democrats are going to have to contend with after the election. The implications will be wide-ranging on everything from the budget, to Israel policy, to how to confront Trump. Can Democratic leaders Sen. Chuck Schumer and Rep. Hakeem Jeffries keep Democrats united? So far, it doesn’t appear so. (Notably, Schumer issued a statement calling it a “despicable betrayal from Rep. Stevens.”).\n\n &#8202; And then, there’s Alaska and the mysterious vanishing Senate candidate, Dan J. Sullivan Jr. The extent to which staff for Democratic Senate nominee Mary Peltola helped install a spoiler candidate (Sullivan Jr., not to be confused with Sen. Dan S. Sullivan, or maybe “to be confused” is the whole point) is now under investigation. Is it a crime? We will see. Is it a mess? Most certainly. Could it affect the outcome of the election in that state? Perhaps most fittingly, polls don’t close in Alaska until 1 a.m. ET Wednesday.\n\n &#8202; This is an edition of the Politics newsletter, bringing you an expert guide to what’s driving D.C. every day. If you’re not subscribed, sign up here . \n\n &#8202; &#8202; &#8202; &#8202; &#8202; About Me &#8202; I’m Damian Paletta, The Wall Street Journal’s Washington coverage chief. I’ve covered Washington for 22 years as a reporter and editor. I’ve covered the White House, Congress, national security, the federal budget, economics and multiple market meltdowns.\n\nWSJ Politics brings you an expert guide to what’s driving D.C., every weekday morning. Send your feedback to politics@wsj.com (if you’re reading this in your inbox, you can just hit reply). This edition was curated and edited in collaboration with Joe Haberstroh. Got a tip for us? Here’s how to submit .\n\n &#8202; &#8202; Unsubscribe Newsletters &#38; Alerts Contact Us Privacy Policy Cookie Policy &#8202; Dow Jones &#38; Company, Inc. 4300 U.S. Route 1 North Monmouth Junction, NJ 08852 You are currently subscribed as teruyuki@hh.iij4u.or.jp . For further assistance, please contact Customer Service at support@‌wsj.com or 1-8‌00-JOUR‌NAL. &#8202; Copyright © 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. | All Rights Reserved. &#8202;",
