color:#0274b6;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-weight:700;font-size:14px;line-height:1.429;letter-spacing:0;font-stretch:normal;text-transform:none;font-style:normal target=_blank rel=\"nofollow noopener\"><span class=\"global-no-link nl-body-headline\"style=\"text-decoration:none;font-family:Times New Roman,serif;font-weight:700;font-size:28px;line-height:1.143;letter-spacing:-.84%;font-stretch:normal;text-transform:none;font-style:normal;color:#111\">Democrats’ Disastrous Final Week Foreshadows Post-Election Drama</span></a></div></td></tr><tr><td style=height:24px;line-height:24px;font-size:0;word-break:break-word class=global-spacer24 height=24><div style=height:0;line-height:0>&#8202;</div></td></tr><tr><td style=font-size:0;padding:0;word-break:break-word class=\"text nl-body-standfirst\"align=left><div style=text-align:left;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-weight:700;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:0;font-stretch:normal;text-transform:none;font-style:normal;color:#111>Dysfunction in Michigan and mystery in Alaska mean party leaders have healing work to do—no matter the results on Tuesday</div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr></tbody></table></div><!--[if mso | IE]><![endif]--></td></tr></tbody></table></div><!--[if mso | IE]><![endif]--></td></tr></tbody></table><table border=0 cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0 role=presentation width=100% align=center bgcolor=#ffffff class=content style=background-color:#fff;width:100%;color:#222><tbody><tr><td><!--[if mso | IE]><table border=0 cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0 role=presentation width=600 align=center class=content-outlook style=width:600px><tr><td style=line-height:0;font-size:0;mso-line-height-rule:exactly><![endif]--><div style=\"margin:0 auto;max-width:600px\"><table border=0 cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0 role=presentation style=width:100% align=center><tbody><tr><td style=direction:ltr;font-size:0;padding:0;text-align:center><!--[if mso | IE]><table border=0 cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0 role=presentation><tr><td style=vertical-align:top;width:600px><![endif]--><div style=font-size:0;text-align:left;direction:ltr;display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;width:100% class=\"mj-outlook-group-fix mj-column-per-100\"><table border=0 cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0 role=presentation width=100%><tbody><tr><td style=vertical-align:top;padding:0><table border=0 cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0 role=presentation width=100%><tbody><tr><td style=height:24px;line-height:24px;font-size:0;word-break:break-word class=global-spacer24 height=24><div style=height:0;line-height:0>&#8202;</div></td></tr><tr><td style=font-size:0;padding:0;word-break:break-word align=center><table border=0 cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0 role=presentation style=border-collapse:collapse;border-spacing:0><tbody><tr><td style=width:600px><img alt=\"Former Rep. Mike Rogers, a Republican, and Rep. Haley Stevens, a Democrat, meet the press Sunday. \"src=\"https://images.wsj.net/im-67603890?width=600&amp;size=1.499\"style=border:0;display:block;outline:0;text-decoration:none;height:auto;width:100%;font-size:13px width=600 height=auto></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr><td style=height:8px;line-height:8px;font-size:0;word-break:break-word class=global-spacer08 height=8><div style=height:0;line-height:0>&#8202;</div></td></tr><tr><td style=font-size:0;padding:0;word-break:break-word align=left><div style=font-family:Ubuntu,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;line-height:1;text-align:left;color:#000><span class=nl-body-caption style=font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-weight:400;font-size:12px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:0;font-stretch:normal;text-transform:none;font-style:normal;color:#6f6f6f>Former Rep. Mike Rogers, a Republican, and Rep. Haley Stevens, a Democrat, meet the press Sunday. EMILY ELCONIN/BLOOMBERG NEWS</span></div></td></tr><tr><td style=height:24px;line-height:24px;font-size:0;word-break:break-word class=global-spacer24 height=24><div style=height:0;line-height:0>&#8202;</div></td></tr><tr><td style=font-size:0;padding:0;word-break:break-word class=text align=left><div style=text-align:left;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-weight:400;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:0;font-stretch:normal;text-transform:none;font-style:normal;color:#222><p style=margin:0><strong>Good morning.</strong> Maybe they win Tuesday. Maybe they win a whole lot. But the major problems Democrats revealed in the past week won’t go away even if there is a Blue Wave.</p></div></td></tr><tr><td style=height:24px;line-height:24px;font-size:0;word-break:break-word class=global-spacer24 height=24><div style=height:0;line-height:0>&#8202;</div></td></tr><tr><td style=font-size:0;padding:0;word-break:break-word class=text align=left><div style=text-align:left;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-weight:400;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:0;font-stretch:normal;text-transform:none;font-style:normal;color:#222><p style=margin:0>This election was never really about a united message from Democrats. Rather, it had turned into a referendum on President Trump, the Iran war, prices, and—to a certain extent—an antitech backlash.</p></div></td></tr><tr><td style=height:24px;line-height:24px;font-size:0;word-break:break-word class=global-spacer24 height=24><div style=height:0;line-height:0>&#8202;</div></td></tr><tr><td style=font-size:0;padding:0;word-break:break-word class=text align=left><div style=text-align:left;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-weight:400;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:0;font-stretch:normal;text-transform:none;font-style:normal;color:#222><p style=margin:0>But hoooooooo boy. In the past week, Democrats have put some of their problems on full display.</p></div></td></tr><tr><td style=height:24px;line-height:24px;font-size:0;word-break:break-word class=global-spacer24 height=24><div style=height:0;line-height:0>&#8202;</div></td></tr><tr><td style=font-size:0;padding:0;word-break:break-word class=text align=left><div style=text-align:left;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-weight:400;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:0;font-stretch:normal;text-transform:none;font-style:normal;color:#222><p style=margin:0>In no place has the dysfunction been as apparent as Michigan. Democratic Rep. Haley Stevens on Sunday endorsed Mike Rogers, the Republican candidate for Senate. This followed two days of “passive aggressive” and then finally “aggressive aggressive” squabbling between Stevens and Abdul El-Sayed, the Democratic Senate candidate.</p></div></td></tr><tr><td style=height:24px;line-height:24px;font-size:0;word-break:break-word class=global-spacer24 height=24><div style=height:0;line-height:0>&#8202;</div></td></tr><tr><td style=font-size:0;padding:0;word-break:break-word class=text align=left><div style=text-align:left;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-weight:400;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:0;font-stretch:normal;text-transform:none;font-style:normal;color:#222><p style=margin:0>El-Sayed barely edged Stevens in the primary earlier this year, and clearly their differences had never been mended. Their rift of new guard vs. old guard is something Democrats are going to have to contend with after the election. The implications will be wide-ranging on everything from the budget, to Israel policy, to how to confront Trump. Can Democratic leaders Sen. Chuck Schumer and Rep. Hakeem Jeffries keep Democrats united? So far, it doesn’t appear so. (Notably, Schumer issued a <a href=\"https://trk.wsj.com/click/48201408.4760/aHR0cHM6Ly94LmNvbS9jaHVja3NjaHVtZXIvc3RhdHVzLzIxMTY5Njk3MDc0MjU0Njg1MzY_bW9kPWRqZW1Qb2xpdGljcw/64d399fc856ebc3ad955d720B0714d1ac\"style=color:#0274b6 target=_blank class=url-no-underline>statement calling</a> it a “despicable betrayal from Rep. Stevens.”).</p></div></td></tr><tr><td style=height:24px;line-height:24px;font-size:0;word-break:break-word class=global-spacer24 height=24><div style=height:0;line-height:0>&#8202;</div></td></tr><tr><td style=font-size:0;padding:0;word-break:break-word class=text align=left><div style=text-align:left;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-weight:400;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:0;font-stretch:normal;text-transform:none;font-style:normal;color:#222><p style=margin:0>And then, there’s Alaska and the mysterious vanishing Senate candidate, Dan J. Sullivan Jr. The extent to which staff for Democratic Senate nominee Mary Peltola helped install a spoiler candidate (Sullivan Jr., not to be confused with Sen. Dan S. Sullivan, or maybe “to be confused” is the whole point) is now under investigation. Is it a crime? We will see. Is it a mess? Most certainly. Could it affect the outcome of the election in that state? Perhaps most fittingly, polls don’t close in Alaska until 1 a.m. ET Wednesday.</p></div></td></tr><tr><td style=height:24px;line-height:24px;font-size:0;word-break:break-word class=global-spacer24 height=24><div style=height:0;line-height:0>&#8202;</div></td></tr><tr><td style=font-size:0;padding:0;word-break:break-word class=text align=left><div style=text-align:left;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-weight:400;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:0;font-stretch:normal;text-transform:none;font-style:normal;color:#222><p style=margin:0><em>This is an edition of the Politics newsletter, bringing you an expert guide to what’s driving D.C. every day. If you’re not subscribed, </em><a href=\"https://trk.wsj.com/click/48201408.4760/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cud3NqLmNvbS9uZXdzbGV0dGVycy93c2otcG9saXRpY3MtcG9saWN5P21vZD1kamVtUG9saXRpY3M/64d399fc856ebc3ad955d720B17a99ef4\"style=color:#0274b6 target=_blank class=url-no-underline><em>sign up here</em></a><em>.</em></p></div></td></tr><tr><td style=height:24px;line-height:24px;font-size:0;word-break:break-word class=global-spacer24 height=24><div style=height:0;line-height:0>&#8202;</div></td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid;border-color:#ccc;font-size:0;word-break:break-word\"class=global-divider040><div style=height:0;line-height:0>&#8202;</div></td></tr><tr><td style=height:24px;line-height:24px;font-size:0;word-break:break-word class=global-spacer24 height=24><div style=height:0;line-height:0>&#8202;</div></td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid;border-color:#ccc;font-size:0;word-break:break-word\"class=global-divider040><div style=height:0;line-height:0>&#8202;</div></td></tr><tr><td style=height:24px;line-height:24px;font-size:0;word-break:break-word class=global-spacer24 height=24><div style=height:0;line-height:0>&#8202;</div></td></tr><tr><td style=font-size:0;padding:0;word-break:break-word class=nl-article-subhed align=left><div style=\"text-align:left;font-family:Times New Roman,serif;font-weight:700;font-size:24px;line-height:1.167;letter-spacing:-.72%;font-stretch:normal;text-transform:none;font-style:normal;color:#111\"><h3 style=margin:0;font-size:inherit>About Me</h3></div></td></tr><tr><td style=height:24px;line-height:24px;font-size:0;word-break:break-word class=global-spacer24 height=24><div style=height:0;line-height:0>&#8202;</div></td></tr><tr><td style=font-size:0;padding:0;word-break:break-word class=text align=left><div style=text-align:left;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-weight:400;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:0;font-stretch:normal;text-transform:none;font-style:normal;color:#222><p style=margin:0>I’m Damian Paletta, The Wall Street Journal’s Washington coverage chief. I’ve covered Washington for 22 years as a reporter and editor. I’ve covered the White House, Congress, national security, the federal budget, economics and multiple market meltdowns.<br><br>WSJ Politics brings you an expert guide to what’s driving D.C., every weekday morning. Send your feedback to <a href=\"mailto:politics@wsj.com?mod=djemPolitics\"style=color:#0274b6 target=_blank class=url-no-underline>politics@wsj.com</a> (if you’re reading this in your inbox, you can just hit reply). This edition was curated and edited in collaboration with Joe Haberstroh. Got a tip for us? 